<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
    xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.29.xsd">

    <!--
      F20. `pos.service_charge.manage` — may this person decide that every dine-in bill at this
      branch grows by a percentage.

      ══ What the screen is ══

      /app/settings/service-charge is a branch's service-charge policy: on or off, a percentage, a
      label the guest reads on the paper, and which channels it applies to. pos-service computes
      the charge from it inside the order transaction and snapshots the rate onto the order.

      Before F20 there was no such screen and no such endpoint. The 2026-08-12 walkthrough
      recorded `Service charge Rs 0.00` printed on the charge page AND on every guest's receipt,
      with a charge-page probe of {tip:false, wallet:false, qr:false, houseAccount:false,
      rounding:false} and `service_charge_paisa` non-zero on 0 of 195 orders. The column, the DTO
      field, the receipt line and finance's 4910 credit had all existed since V1 — nothing could
      ever make the number move.

      ══ Why this is NOT pos.menu.manage, and NOT pos.tax.manage ══

      The three decisions only look alike.

        pos.menu.manage    - "this curry is Rs 1,450". Menu work, done a dozen times a week, and
          MANAGER holds it (changeset 041). It re-prices ONE dish.

        pos.tax.manage     - "standard rate is 17%". A statutory fact about the business
          (changeset 091). A service charge is not a tax: it is the restaurant's own money,
          credited to its own revenue account 4910, and calling it a tax to reuse a permission
          would put a commercial decision behind a fiscal gate.

        pos.service_charge.manage - "every dine-in bill here grows by 5%". It re-prices every
          check in the building at once, including checks already open, it is what the guest
          argues with the manager about, and there is no reason code and no per-check record of
          who decided it. That belongs with the person who owns the pricing.

      Phase 19b established this precedent when it split pos.tables.admin from pos.tables.manage,
      28-04 repeated it for pos.terminals.admin and 091 for pos.tax.manage: verbs that share a
      noun and nothing else do not share a permission.

      ══ Who holds it ══

      OWNER and TENANT_ADMIN - the same two personas /app/settings already admits (a branch
      MANAGER is refused there today), and the same holder set changesets 089, 090 and 091 chose
      for the same reason. If it turns out a branch manager needs it, the fix is another explicit
      grant argued on its own, not a widening of what this code means.

      MANAGER is deliberately left able to READ the policy: ServiceChargeServiceImpl.get() gates
      on pos.menu.view, because the charge appears on every bill they hand a guest and a manager
      who cannot look up the rate is being asked to defend a number the product hides from them.

      This widens nothing. Before this changeset no role could set a service charge, because no
      screen and no endpoint existed that could.

      ══ Why the grant is explicit ══

      Same reason 055, 059, 082, 083, 085, 088, 089, 090 and 091 each give: the blanket "grant
      every pos.* permission to OWNER" statements in changeset 041 are point-in-time and
      retro-grant nothing added after they ran. A permission that no role holds does not fail at
      startup, does not break a test, and produces a clean confident 403 for every user including
      OWNER - which is indistinguishable from a deliberate denial. The verification changeSet
      below RAISEs rather than warns for exactly that reason.
    -->

    <changeSet id="auth-1.0.0-093-pos-service-charge-manage" author="restaurantos-agent" runOnChange="false">
        <comment>pos.service_charge.manage — set a branch's service-charge percentage; held by OWNER and TENANT_ADMIN</comment>
        <sql splitStatements="true" endDelimiter=";">
            INSERT INTO permissions (code, module, description) VALUES
              ('pos.service_charge.manage', 'pos', 'Set the service-charge percentage every check at a branch is billed')
            ON CONFLICT (code) DO NOTHING;

            INSERT INTO role_permissions (role_code, permission_code) VALUES
              ('OWNER',        'pos.service_charge.manage'),
              ('TENANT_ADMIN', 'pos.service_charge.manage')
            ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
        </sql>

        <rollback>
            <sql splitStatements="true" endDelimiter=";">
                DELETE FROM role_permissions WHERE permission_code = 'pos.service_charge.manage';
                DELETE FROM permissions WHERE code = 'pos.service_charge.manage';
            </sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="auth-1.0.0-093-verify-pos-service-charge-manage" author="restaurantos-agent" runOnChange="false">
        <comment>Fail the migration if pos.service_charge.manage exists but no role holds it</comment>
        <sql splitStatements="false">
            DO $$
            DECLARE holder_count INT;
            BEGIN
                SELECT count(*) INTO holder_count
                  FROM role_permissions
                 WHERE permission_code = 'pos.service_charge.manage';
                IF holder_count = 0 THEN
                    RAISE EXCEPTION
                      'pos.service_charge.manage is defined but granted to no role. pos-service refuses every write to /api/v1/pos/branches/{id}/service-charge without it, so the service-charge screen would render an access refusal for every user on every tenant including OWNER - as an ordinary-looking permission denial, not an error anyone would trace back to this migration.';
                END IF;
            END $$;
        </sql>
        <rollback/>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
